From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: v8: turboshaft drop SilenceNaN when the value came out of IEEE arithmetic

V8 inserts SilenceNaN wherever a double is about to be written into a
double-representation array or field.  The reason is not IEEE hygiene, it is
that V8 spends two signalling NaN bit patterns as in-band sentinels:

  kHoleNanInt64      = 0xFFF7FFFF'FFF7FFFF   the hole in a FixedDoubleArray
  kUndefinedNanInt64 = 0xFFF6FFFF'FFF6FFFF   undefined in a double array
                                             (v8_enable_undefined_double,
                                             true by default in this Chromium)

Both have bit 51, the fraction MSB, clear, i.e. both are signalling.  Script
can get hold of an arbitrary signalling NaN -- write one through a
Uint32Array and read the same buffer back as a Float64Array -- so without
silencing, `a[i] = thatValue` on a double array would let script forge a
hole or an undefined.  Silencing sets bit 51, which lands the value outside
both sentinels while preserving sign and payload.

The cost is per store.  On ppc64 it is MacroAssembler::CanonicalizeNaN,
which is LoadDoubleLiteral(kDoubleRegZero, 0.0) followed by fsub.  On this
target V8_EMBEDDED_CONSTANT_POOL_BOOL is true, so that is an lfd out of the
constant pool plus a constant pool slot, plus the fsub, and it clobbers d14.
kDoubleRegZero is not maintained as 0.0 anywhere on ppc64 -- CanonicalizeNaN
is its only user, and the register is also handed out as an FP scratch via
Assembler::DefaultFPTmpList -- so the load is not removable on its own.
x64 pays xorpd+subsd; arm64 pays a single fsub against its reserved fp_zero.

IEEE 754-2019 makes most of these stores unnecessary.  Clause 7.2 requires a
general-computational operation handed a signalling NaN to signal invalid
and deliver a *quiet* NaN, and clause 6.2 requires a NaN produced by an
invalid operation to be quiet as well.  Addition, subtraction,
multiplication, division and squareRoot are general-computational, and every
V8 backend lowers them to the one hardware instruction the architecture
documents to those rules.  So the result of `x/y` is never a signalling NaN
no matter what x and y were, and SilenceNaN of it is a no-op.

This adds that reduction to MachineOptimizationReducer::REDUCE(FloatUnary):
SilenceNaN(v) folds to v when v is a FloatBinop of kind kAdd, kSub, kMul or
kDiv, or a FloatUnary of kind kSqrt, at the same FloatRepresentation.
kSilenceNaN itself is included too, since it is idempotent.

What V8 already knew, and what is new here
------------------------------------------
TurboFan's simplified lowering already drops the node when the value cannot
be a NaN at all: the kNumberSilenceNaN case in simplified-lowering.cc tests
`input_type.Is(Type::OrderedNumber())` and replaces the node with its input.
That is the better mechanism for that case and this patch does not duplicate
it -- notably it does not try to cover integer-to-double conversions, which
the type system already handles.

The case the type system cannot reach is the one here: a value that *can* be
NaN, so the type is Number and the node survives, but whose NaN can only
ever be quiet because it came out of an arithmetic unit.  `a[i] = p/q` on a
PACKED_DOUBLE_ELEMENTS array is exactly that, and it is the common shape --
the insertion site is js-native-context-specialization.cc's keyed store
("Make sure we do not store signalling NaNs into double arrays"), with
js-create-lowering.cc and js-call-reducer.cc behind it.

Doing it in Turboshaft rather than in the TurboFan simplified graph also
picks up the Turbolev path: turbolev-graph-builder.cc maps both
maglev::Float64ToSilencedFloat64 and maglev::ChangeFloat64ToHoleyFloat64 to
Float64SilenceNaN with no typing in front of them, so a Maglev-origin graph
has no equivalent of the OrderedNumber check at all.

MachineOptimizationReducer sits in the MachineLoweringPhase reducer stack
below MachineLoweringReducer, so it sees the SilenceNaN ops that machine
lowering emits for stores as they are emitted, and it runs again in the
load-elimination and memory-optimization phases.

What is deliberately left out
-----------------------------
The in-code comment lists these; the reasoning in short:

  Abs, Negate.  Clause 5.5.1 sign bit operations, not arithmetic: they copy
  the operand and set or flip the sign bit, and a signalling NaN stays
  signalling.  The FloatBinop reducer in this same file already depends on
  this -- it refuses to rewrite `x * -1` as `-x` when signalling NaNs are
  possible, with that exact comment.  Confirmed on hardware: Math.abs of
  0x7FF00000'00000001 reads back unchanged, and unary minus returns
  0xFFF00000'00000001.

  Min, Max.  These cannot create a NaN, only propagate an input one, and
  whether the propagation quiets is a property of eight separate lowerings
  rather than of the operation: x64's kSSEFloat64Min branches to
  OutOfLineLoadFloat64NaN on unordered, ppc64's MacroAssembler::MinF64 and
  MaxF64 route the unordered case through fadd, arm64 emits Fmin/Fmax and
  leans on FPProcessNaNs.  They all quiet today.  That is not the same as a
  guarantee, and min/max of a NaN is rare enough in a store that it is not
  worth the audit.

  Mod, Power, and the transcendentals.  These are calls -- on ppc64,
  kPPC_ModDouble is a CallCFunction to mod_two_doubles_operation and the
  kIeee754* opcodes call into base::ieee754 -- and a libm is entitled to
  return its NaN argument unchanged.  The SilenceNaN is also noise next to
  the call.

  RoundDown, RoundUp, RoundToZero, RoundTiesEven.  roundToIntegral* are
  general-computational, so the IEEE argument does apply, but the premise
  that each backend implements them in one instruction does not: RISC-V's
  MacroAssembler::RoundHelper fmv_d's the operand straight to the
  destination for the NaN/Inf case and then needs an explicit
  fmin_d(dst, src, src) to canonicalise it.  That is a sequence whose
  correctness has to be read per backend, not an appeal to the standard, so
  the family stays out.  This is a narrowing, not a claim that it is unsafe.

  Float32 to Float64 conversion.  convertFormat is general-computational,
  but ppc64 keeps single precision in double format and lowers
  kPPC_Float32ToDouble to a plain register Move, which does not quiet.  A
  concrete counterexample, so conversions stay out.

Why phase ordering cannot defeat it
-----------------------------------
The reducer's own algebraic identities fire on the input before the
SilenceNaN is reduced, because a CopyingPhase has already mapped and reduced
the input by then.  So `SilenceNaN(x * 1.0)` has become `SilenceNaN(x)` by
the time this code runs, and `SilenceNaN(x * -1.0)` has become
`SilenceNaN(FloatNegate(x))`.  In both cases the match simply does not fire
and the SilenceNaN is kept -- which is the correct outcome, since neither x
nor FloatNegate(x) is guaranteed quiet.  The ordering can only cost a missed
optimisation, never correctness.

The reduction does not consult signalling_nan_possible (which is
data()->is_wasm()).  It does not need to: the claim is about what the
operation produces, not about what reaches it.  Every producer of
Float64SilenceNaN is a JS-pipeline or CSA site anyway -- machine lowering's
JS primitive conversions and double-array stores, the TurboFan graph
builder, Turbolev, and CodeStubAssembler -- none of them a wasm graph
builder.

Bit-exactness
-------------
The elision is not merely "quiet either way", it is bit-for-bit invisible.
On every backend that implements SilenceNaN as a subtraction of zero
(ppc64 fsub, x64 subsd, arm64 fsub), subtracting zero from an already-quiet
NaN propagates it unchanged, so removing the operation changes nothing.  On
RISC-V, where arithmetic canonicalises every NaN result to
0x7FF8000000000000, the producing add/sub/mul/div/sqrt has already
canonicalised the value before SilenceNaN would have seen it, so again
nothing changes.  The only side effect dropped is the IEEE invalid flag that
the subtraction would have raised on a signalling input -- and after
arithmetic the input is never signalling, so that flag was never raised.
JS does not observe the FP status flags in any case.

Arch-independent, and upstreamable
----------------------------------
This touches only shared Turboshaft code; there is no ppc64 content in it,
and nothing in it is specific to this port.  It is an upstream candidate
rather than a port fix.  It is filed here because this is where the series
lives, and because the ppc64 lowering is the most expensive of the three
(constant pool load plus fsub plus a clobbered scratch register).

It is NOT wired into the PKGBUILD.  It has not been compiled and it has not
been benchmarked; nothing in this tree has built it.  The identifiers it
uses were checked against this exact tree -- FloatBinopOp::Kind has kAdd,
kMul, kMin, kMax, kSub, kDiv, kMod, kPower, kAtan2; FloatUnaryOp::Kind has
kSqrt and kSilenceNaN; both ops carry a `rep` field; OperationMatcher::
TryCast is already used in this file -- but that is not a compile.

Behavioural baseline
--------------------
The observable contract was measured against node 26.4.0 (V8 14.6) on
ppc64le before the patch was written, by loading a signalling NaN out of a
Float64Array by index and reading the result back through a second
Float64Array (a typed-array store is the one channel that does not silence).
Everything was done inside functions warmed to the optimising tiers, because
a NaN that the compiler can see as a constant folds to a canonical quiet NaN
and tells you nothing.  Starting from 0x7FF00000'00000001:

  no store, in optimised code
    x+0, x+1, x-1, x*2, x/3, Math.sqrt(x)   ->  7FF80000'00000001  quieted
    Math.abs(x)                             ->  7FF00000'00000001  NOT quieted
    -x                                      ->  FFF00000'00000001  NOT quieted
    Math.min(x,1), Math.max(x,1)            ->  7FF80000'00000001  quieted
    Math.floor(x), x%7                      ->  7FF80000'00000001  quieted
    x-0, x*1, x/1                           ->  7FF00000'00000001  NOT quieted

  stored into a packed or holey double array (identical for both)
    all of the above                        ->  7FF80000'00000001
    -x                                      ->  FFF80000'00000001
    x**3, Math.fround(x)                    ->  7FF80000'00000000

  with an already-quiet 7FF80000'00000003 going in, every store comes back
  7FF80000'00000003 (FFF80000'00000003 through unary minus), payload intact

  sentinels, stored with and without arithmetic in front
    hole NaN      ->  FFFFFFFF'FFF7FFFF   quiet, not a hole, typeof number
    undefined NaN ->  FFFEFFFF'FFF6FFFF   quiet, not undefined
    the untouched hole of a holey array still reads undefined

  not silenced at all (the control, without which nothing can fail)
    Float64Array element, object property   ->  7FF00000'00000001

The x-0, x*1 and x/1 line is worth dwelling on, because it is the strongest
evidence that this patch is in keeping with what V8 already does: those three
come back *signalling* in optimised code because MachineOptimizationReducer
deletes the operation outright (lhs - +0.0 => lhs, lhs * 1 => lhs,
lhs / 1 => lhs), guarded only by !signalling_nan_possible, which is false for
all of JS.  Under --jitless the interpreter performs the subtraction and the
NaN comes back quiet.  V8 has therefore already accepted a script-observable
deviation of exactly this kind; the hole and undefined sentinels are the only
place the quiet bit is load-bearing, and that place keeps its SilenceNaN.

Every expectation above is invariant under this patch.  The operations it
elides have already quieted the NaN themselves, so the bits do not move; the
operations it does not elide are where the store is what quiets.  The
discriminating cases are Math.abs and unary minus: if either were ever added
to the elided set, their stored results would come back with bit 51 clear.

The battery in v8set-test/test.js was extended with 91 checks covering all of
the above -- the control that proves a signalling NaN is visible from script
at all, the direct and the stored tables for packed and for holey double
arrays, the quiet-NaN payload passthrough, the two sentinels, and NaN and
signed-zero propagation through min/max/abs/negate.  It reports 191 checks,
0 failures on node 26.4.0 with the default flags and with --no-maglev.

